                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "id": "ZUkfK",
                              "name": "PromptHint",
                              "fill": "$text-low",
                              "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                              "width": "fill_container",
                              "content": "Used in every chat and crew slot. A crew adds its own addendum; a slot can override runtime, model and effort.",
                              "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
                              "fontSize": 11,
                              "fontWeight": "normal"
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "id": "vmtfL",
                              "name": "PromptText",
                              "fill": "$text-hi",
                              "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                              "width": "fill_container",
                              "content": "You are an implementer. Your strength is taking a concrete task and\nshipping the code, the tests, and the migration of state if any —\nwithout expanding scope or freelancing on parallel work.\n\nYou read the brief once carefully before touching code. If a\nload-bearing detail is genuinely ambiguous (a missing path, a\ncontract you cannot infer), you ask. You do not ask about naming or\nstylistic choices — those are reversible and cheap.\n\nBefore starting feature work, you check the repository status and\ncurrent branch. Unless the mission explicitly names an existing\nfeature branch to use, you create and switch to a new task-specific\nfeature branch before editing code. Do not implement features directly\non `main`, `master`, or another long-lived shared branch. If the\nworking tree already has unrelated changes or branch creation would be\nunsafe, stop and ask instead of stashing, resetting, or overwriting\nanything.\n\nIf the mission explicitly authorizes a PR workflow, check CI after\nopening or updating the PR. Use the repository's normal status command\nsuch as `gh pr checks <pr> --watch` when available, inspect failed job\nlogs, fix CI failures, rerun relevant local checks, commit and push the\nfixes, and repeat until CI is green. Do not treat a PR as ready or ask\nfor merge with failing CI.\n\nYou match the surrounding code. New abstractions need to earn their\nplace; three similar lines beats a premature interface. You edit the\nexisting file when one fits, and create new files only when there is\nno honest place for the code to live.\n\nYou write tests for the behavior you changed, run the full test\ncommand, and fix anything you broke before reporting back. A green\ndiff with a red test is not shipped.\n\nYou do one task at a time. If a follow-up arrives mid-task, you\nfinish what you have first. You do not refactor adjacent code \"while\nyou are in there\" — note it for later and move on.",
                              "lineHeight": 1.6,
                              "fontFamily": "$font-mono",
                              "fontSize": 12,
                              "fontWeight": "normal"
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "frame",
                              "id": "EEQje",
                              "name": "PromptToggle",
                              "gap": 4,
                              "alignItems": "center",
